<p><strong>To price a spa or salon service, work out what it costs you in time, staff, products and space, add the margin you need, then check it against nearby businesses. Then decide how packages, memberships and discounts will change that price.</strong></p>
<h2>1. Start from the cost of one service</h2>
<p>List what a single service uses: the therapist or stylist's time, products, room time and a share of rent and running costs. A service that looks cheap to sell can be expensive to deliver if it blocks a room for an hour.</p>
<h2>2. Set a floor you will not go below</h2>
<p>Decide the lowest price at which a service still earns something. Every discount, festival offer and package should stay above that floor.</p>
<h2>3. Look at nearby prices, then decide your position</h2>
<p>Check what similar businesses nearby charge. You do not have to match them, but you should know why you are higher or lower.</p>
<h2>4. Use packages and memberships on purpose</h2>
<p>A package of sessions or a prepaid membership gives the client a better rate and gives you cash upfront. Fix the discount before you sell it. See <a href="/features/packages/">packages</a>, <a href="/features/memberships/">memberships</a> and <a href="/features/vouchers/">gift vouchers</a>, or read <a href="/blog/spa-memberships-packages-vouchers-nepal/">how spas run memberships, packages and vouchers</a>.</p>
<h2>5. Control who can discount</h2>
<p>The price you set is only the price if staff follow it. Decide who may give a discount and who approves it. See <a href="/features/discount-management/">discount management</a> and <a href="/blog/discount-leakage/">discount leakage</a>.</p>
<h2>6. Review prices with real numbers</h2>
<p>Look at which services earn the most and which therapists or stylists they depend on. <a href="/features/reports/">Reports</a> show revenue by service and staff so price changes are not guesswork.</p>
